{
 "id": "2025-liu-landscape",
 "name": "Landscape Aesthetic Quality – Tree Image Ranking & Forest Rating (Jiuzhaigou)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Liu et al. (2025), \"Landscape Aesthetics Quality in Subalpine Forests of Eastern Tibetan Plateau Will Greatly Decrease by the End of the Century?\", Forests 16:1804. This is the paper's full online questionnaire: four background questions; six series of tree images (questionnaire Q5–Q10, 3–4 images each, labelled A–D) that participants rank from most to least preferred; then ten autumn forest-stand photographs from Jiuzhaigou (Q11–Q20) rated from 1 (very ugly) to 5 (very beautiful). Each tree series varied one attribute of the eight dominant species (such as crown shape, leaf shape or colour) and participants were not told the species. In the original study 532 people answered; the species scores derived from the rankings agreed closely with the stand ratings (R = 0.925) and were used to map landscape aesthetic quality, which the authors project to decline sharply by 2096.\n\nImages and wording: Liu et al. (2025), supplementary questionnaire, CC BY 4.0. Used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) English only (original Chinese Wenjuanxing questionnaire); (2) images are the versions printed in the supplement, some only 250–380 px wide; (3) ranking is done by tapping images in order instead of the Wenjuanxing ranking widget; (4) the 5–10 s viewing time is an instruction, as in the original, not enforced; (5) a consent checkbox was added. The attribute each tree series varies is inferred from the images and noted in the trial data.",
